ivision by zero\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: cyclomatic_per_loc\n# Feature description: Cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) normalized by the number of lines of code (loc).\n# Usefulness: This feature helps measure how complex the logic is relative to the size of the module. High cyclomatic complexity per line of code could indicate overly intricate logic, which may increase the chance of defects.\ndf['cyclomatic_per_loc'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)  # Adding small value to avoid division by zero\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped\n# The column 'locCodeAndComment' has very limited variation in the dataset (mostly zeros with a mean close to zero and very low standard deviation). This lack of information makes it unlikely to be useful for prediction and it introduces unnecessary noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\n# The column 'l' (Halstead program length) has a very narrow range (0 to 2) and its implications for software defects are unclear or redundant when combined with other Halstead metrics like 'n' and 'v'. Dropping it reduces redundancy in the feature set.\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column b is dropped\n# The column 'b' (Halstead error estimate) has minimal variance in the dataset (mean ~0.089, std ~0.176), indicating it does not provide meaningful discriminative power for a binary classification task of defect prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['b'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
