###["python\n# 1. Halstead Difficulty-to-Volume Ratio\n# Creating a new feature that computes the ratio of Halstead's difficulty (d) to volume (v): d/v.\n# Usefulness: This ratio can help identify modules where the difficulty of comprehension or modification might not scale proportionally with its size or complexity. High values could signal poor maintainability or potential for defects.\ndf['difficulty_volume_ratio'] = df['d'] / (df['v'] + 1e-8)  # Adding small epsilon to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\npython\n# 2. Normalized Cyclomatic Complexity\n# Creating a new feature by normalizing cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) by lines of code (loc): v(g)/loc.\n# Usefulness: Cyclomatic complexity per line of code provides a measure of logical complexity density. Modules with higher density might indicate hotspots for potential defects.\ndf['norm_cyclomatic_complexity'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-8)  # Adding small epsilon to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\npython\n# 3. Halstead Volume-to-Operators Ratio\n# Creating a new feature that computes the ratio of Halstead volume (v) to the total number of operators (total_Op): v/total_Op.\n# Usefulness: This ratio approximates the information content (volume) per operation in the code. It helps detect overly dense or verbose modules—both of which can be associated with higher defect risks.\ndf['volume_per_operator'] = df['v'] / (df['total_Op'] + 1e-8)  # Adding small epsilon to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping locCodeAndComment column\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped:\n# This column is not meaningful for predicting defects as it is populated with all zeros in the dataset, showing no variability to extract useful information for the downstream task.\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping lOComment and lOBlank columns\n# Explanation why the columns lOComment and lOBlank are dropped:\n# These features mostly contain zeros (median is 0.0), offering little value for capturing variability in the dataset. Since comments and blank lines have limited direct correlation with software defects compared to executable code, these can be safely dropped.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment', 'lOBlank'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping b column (Halstead 'Bugs' approximation)\n# Explanation why the column b is dropped:\n# Halstead's 'Bugs' estimation (b) could introduce redundancy, as other Halstead metrics like difficulty (d), effort (e), and intelligence (i) already encapsulate details about the complexity and maintainability of the code. Including b risks collinearity.\ndf.drop(columns=['b'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nThese added features and dropped columns aim to enhance the predictive power of the model by introducing meaningful ratios that describe code complexity, maintainability, and density, while removing irrelevant or redundant features. This approach balances extracting useful insights and maintaining dataset simplicity.", "### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering:\n\npython\n# Feature name: normalized_volume_per_operator\n# Feature description: Halstead's volume (v) divided by the number of unique operators (uniq_Op).\n# Usefulness: This feature indicates the density of the information volume in relation to the variety of operators used. High values may suggest dense, less readable code, which could correlate with higher defect risks.\ndf['normalized_volume_per_operator'] = df['v'] / (df['uniq_Op'] + 1e-8)  # Adding small epsilon to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: logical_density\n# Feature description: Cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) per Halstead's line count (lOCode).\n# Usefulness: Captures the density of logical complexity relative to actual lines of code, highlighting modules with high complexity in small amounts of code, which may make them error-prone.\ndf['logical_density'] = df['iv(g)'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1e-5)  # Adding a small value to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: essential_complexity_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of McCabe's essential complexity (ev(g)) to design complexity (iv(g)).\n# Usefulness: Measures how much of the module complexity is essential, providing insights into whether the complexity is inherent or a result of poor design. Higher values may suggest fundamental challenges in the software design.\ndf['essential_complexity_ratio'] = df['ev(g)'] / (df['iv(g)'] + 1e-8)  # Adding small epsilon to avoid division by zero\nend\n\n### Column Drops\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\n# The column 'lOBlank' has a median and Q3 of 0.0, indicating it is mostly zero for the majority of modules. Its lack of variability and relevance to defect prediction make it uninformative for the task.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ratio_comments_code is dropped\n# The column 'ratio_comments_code' has very low variation (mean close to zero, with Q1, Median, and Q3 all being 0.0), suggesting it adds minimal information for predicting defects while potentially introducing noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['ratio_comments_code'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\n# The column 'ev(g)' (essential complexity) is being replaced with the newly engineered feature 'essential_complexity_ratio', making this column redundant. Dropping it reduces feature redundancy in the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature name: sqrt_loc\n# Feature description: Square root transformation of the 'loc' feature (line count of code).\n# Usefulness: Square root transformation helps reduce skewness in data distribution while maintaining sensitivity to small variations in smaller modules, which might be predictive of defects.\ndf['sqrt_loc'] = np.sqrt(df['loc'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: uniq_Op_density\n# Feature description: Density of unique operators per Halstead's line count (uniq_Op / lOCode).\n# Usefulness: High density of unique operators relative to lines of code may indicate overly intricate logic or densely packed code patterns, which could correlate with higher defect risks.\ndf['uniq_Op_density'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1e-5)  # Adding a small value to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: complexity_interaction\n# Feature description: Interaction term between design complexity (iv(g)) and Halstead intelligence (i): iv(g) * i.\n# Usefulness: Captures the combined effect of design complexity and intelligence on defect likelihood. Complex designs with higher intelligence scores might be prone to defects due to their intricate nature.\ndf['complexity_interaction'] = df['iv(g)'] * df['i']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOComment is dropped\n# The column 'lOComment' has limited variability (median = 0.0), offers little predictive value, and has a weak correlation with features directly related to code complexity or defects.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\n# The column 'lOBlank' has limited variability (median = 0.0, range mostly close to zero), making it redundant and unlikely to contribute meaningful insights for defect prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\n# The column 'ev(g)' is indirectly represented through the engineered feature 'evg_per_ivg', which provides normalized insights into essential complexity relative to design complexity, making 'ev(g)' redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature name: sqrt_loc\n# Feature description: Square root transformation of the 'loc' feature (line count of code).\n# Usefulness: The square root transformation helps reduce skewness in the data distribution while preserving relative differences between smaller and larger modules. This can improve the model's sensitivity to smaller modules, which might be disproportionately defect-prone.\ndf['sqrt_loc'] = np.sqrt(df['loc'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: uniq_Op_density\n# Feature description: Ratio of unique operators (uniq_Op) to Halstead’s line count (lOCode).\n# Usefulness: Captures the density of unique operators in the source code, which can indicate overly intricate logic. High operator density may correlate with defect-prone modules due to increased complexity in a smaller codebase.\ndf['uniq_Op_density'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1e-8)  # Adding small epsilon to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: complexity_interaction\n# Feature description: Interaction term between design complexity (iv(g)) and Halstead intelligence (i): iv(g) * i.\n# Usefulness: This interaction captures the combined effect of the logical design complexity and code intelligence. Highly intricate designs combined with high intelligence scores may contribute to defects due to their intricate and less maintainable nature.\ndf['complexity_interaction'] = df['iv(g)'] * df['i']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOComment is dropped\n# The column 'lOComment' has very limited variability (median = 0.0 and mean close to zero). It provides little value for capturing variability in defect prediction and has a weak correlation with code complexity or defects.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\n# The column 'lOBlank' shows minimal variability (median = 0.0, Q3 = 2.0), making it uninformative for defect prediction. Similarly, its relevance to defect prediction is negligible.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped\n# The column 'locCodeAndComment' primarily contains zeros with no variability, making it redundant. It provides no meaningful contribution to defect prediction and introduces unnecessary noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering:\n\npython\n# Feature name: sqrt_loc\n# Feature description: Square root transformation of the 'loc' feature (line count of code).\n# Usefulness: Square root transformation reduces data skewness while preserving relative differences, especially for smaller modules. This transformation can better capture the relationship between module size and defect incidence.\ndf['sqrt_loc'] = np.sqrt(df['loc'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: intelligence_density\n# Feature description: Halstead's intelligence measure (i) divided by Halstead's line count (lOCode).\n# Usefulness: This feature reflects intelligence per line of code, with higher values potentially indicating efficient, less verbose code that might reduce defect likelihood. It complements intelligence_per_line by using lOCode.\ndf['intelligence_density'] = df['i'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1e-8)  # Adding small epsilon to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: interaction_complexity_volume\n# Feature description: Interaction between design complexity (iv(g)) and Halstead volume (v): iv(g) * v.\n# Usefulness: Combines structural complexity and the overall size of the codebase into a single feature. Modules with high complexity and high volume are more prone to defects due to their intricate nature.\ndf['interaction_complexity_volume'] = df['iv(g)'] * df['v']\nend\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant or Redundant Columns:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\n# The column 'lOBlank' has very low variability (median = 0.0, Q3 = 2.0) and limited relevance to defect prediction. Its information is redundant when considering other meaningful features like loc or lOCode.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped\n# The column 'locCodeAndComment' is populated predominantly with zeros and shows no significant variance. It fails to provide meaningful information for predicting defects and introduces noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\n# The column 'ev(g)' is subsumed by the feature evg_per_ivg, which already represents essential complexity in a normalized and more informative manner. Keeping 'ev(g)' introduces redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
