te nature and larger size.\ndf['interaction_design_volume'] = df['iv(g)'] * df['v']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: intelligence_density\n# Feature description: Halstead's 'intelligence' (i) divided by Halstead's line count (lOCode).\n# Usefulness: Captures intelligence per line of code, providing insights into code density and efficiency. Modules with higher intelligence density are less verbose and better-designed, potentially reducing defect likelihood.\ndf['intelligence_density'] = df['i'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1e-8)  # Adding a small epsilon to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\n# The column 'lOBlank' has very low variability (median = 0.0, Q3 = 2.0) and limited relevance to defect prediction. Most values are zero, making it uninformative, while its information is likely redundant given other meaningful features (loc or lOCode).\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped\n# The column 'locCodeAndComment' consists predominantly of zeros and lacks variability. It fails to contribute meaningful information or insights for predicting defects and introduces unnecessary noise to the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\n# The column 'ev(g)' (essential complexity) is represented more informatively in the derived feature 'evg_per_ivg'. Keeping 'ev(g)' introduces redundancy, increasing the risk of collinearity, and offers no new information.\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\nend"), with improvements -
